To understand why the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) enjoys favorable sample complexity compared to the Wasserstein metrics, we first derive an equivalent form that is more algebraically explicit. Indeed,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{MMD}_k^2(\mu,\nu) = \|\phi_\mu - \phi_\nu\|_{\mathcal{H}_k}^2 &= \langle \phi_\mu - \phi_\nu, \phi_\mu - \phi_\nu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k} \\ &= \langle \phi_\mu, \phi_\mu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k} - 2\langle \phi_\mu, \phi_\nu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k} + \langle \phi_\nu, \phi_\nu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\tag{2.7}
$$

where, considering for example the term $\langle \phi_\mu, \phi_\nu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k}$ , we have from the fact that $\phi_\nu$ is the Riesz representer of $\mathrm{I}_\nu$ and (2.3) that

$$
\begin{aligned}
\langle \phi_\nu, \phi_\mu \rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k} = \mathrm{I}_\nu(\phi_\mu) &= \int \phi_\mu(y) \, \mathrm{d}\nu(y) \\ &= \iint k(x,y) \, \mathrm{d}\mu(x)\mathrm{d}\nu(y).
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\tag{2.8}
$$

Proceeding similarly with all three terms in (2.7) results in the explicit expression

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{MMD}_k^2(\mu,\nu) &= \iint k(x,y)\mathrm{d}\mu(x)\mathrm{d}\mu(y) - 2\iint k(x,y)\mathrm{d}\mu(x)\mathrm{d}\nu(y) \\ &\quad + \iint k(x,y)\mathrm{d}\nu(x)\mathrm{d}\nu(y).
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\tag{2.9}
$$

In settings where the integrals in (2.9) cannot be exactly computed, but where it is nevertheless possible to sample from $\mu$ and $\mu$ , the MMD could be approximated by replacing the intractable kernel mean elements $\phi_\mu$ and $\phi_\nu$ with their respective Monte Carlo estimators $\phi_\mu^n$ and $\phi_\nu^n$ , respectively based on independent samples $X_1,\dots,X_n \sim \mu$ and $Y_1,\dots,Y_n \sim \nu$ , as defined in (2.4). From our observation following (2.4) that $\phi_\mu^n$ can be interpreted as the *exact* kernel mean element corresponding to the approximating measure $\mu_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{X_i}$ , it follows that the plug-in estimator $\|\phi_\mu^n - \phi_\nu^n\|_{\mathcal{H}_k}$ carries the dual interpretation of computing the *exact* $\text{MMD}_k(\mu_n,\nu_n)$ between the two approximating measures $\mu_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{X_i}$ and $\nu_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^n \delta_{Y_j}$ . From this perspective, it is trivial to obtain conditions under which the approximation to MMD obtained in this manner is consistent. Indeed, from (2.9) we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{MMD}_k^2(\mu_n,\nu_n) &= \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{i'=1}^n k(X_i,X_{i'}) - 2\frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^n k(X_i,Y_j) \\ &\quad + \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{j=1}^n\sum_{j'=1}^n k(Y_j,Y_{j'})
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\tag{2.10}
$$

where each of these three terms represents a Monte Carlo approximation of the corresponding integral in (2.9) whose error converges at $O_P(n^{-1/2})$ . As such, the sample complexity of MMD is essentially dimension-independent. Furthermore, the apparent $O(n^2)$ computational complexity associated with the Monte Carlo estimator (2.10) can be mitigated by noting that computation is "embarrassingly parallel".

