$$
\mathbb{E}e^{t(f-\mathbb{E}f)} \le e^{t^2\|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f\|_\infty^2/2} \tag{8}
$$

for any $f$ bounded such that $\|\nabla f\|_\infty < +\infty$ . The Bienaymé-Chebyshev inequality gives,

$$
\mathbb{P}(f - \mathbb{E}f \ge x) \le \inf_{t>0} e^{-tx} \mathbb{E}e^{t(f-\mathbb{E}f)} = e^{-x^2/2\|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f\|_\infty^2}, \tag{9}
$$

for all $x > 0$ . Finally, to remove the boundedness assumption, approximate $f$ by

$$
f_n = f\mathbb{1}_{|f|<n} + |n|\mathbb{1}_{|f|\ge n}. \tag{10}
$$

Then $|f_n(x) - f_n(y)| \le |f(x) - f(y)|$ , $\|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f_n\|_\infty \le \|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f\|_\infty$ , while $\mathbb{E}f_n \to \mathbb{E}f$ . $\quad \square$

**Remark 2.** (i) In the inequality (1), we then are often left with the problem of estimating $\|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f\|_\infty^2$ . A generic estimate is obtained via the weak second moment of $X$ , i.e., via

$$
\|\Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f\|_\infty^2 \le \lambda^* \|\nabla f\|_\infty^2, \tag{11}
$$

where $\lambda^*$ is the maximal eigenvalue of $\Sigma$ :

$$
\lambda^* = \sup_{\|x\|=1} \langle \Sigma x, x \rangle = \sup_{\|x\|=1} \mathbb{E}|\langle X, x \rangle|^2. \tag{12}
$$

(ii) In some instances, e.g., for some particular classes of functions, the weak moment of order two of $X$ can be replaced by the strong second moment, i.e., by $\sigma^{*2} = \max_{i=1,\dots,d} \sigma_{i,i} = \max_{i=1,\dots,d} \operatorname{Var} X_i$ . A case at hand is $f(x) = \max_{1\le i\le d} x_i$ . Indeed, then,

$$
\langle \Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f(x), \Sigma^{1/2}\nabla f(x) \rangle = \sum_{i=1}^d \sum_{j=1}^d \sigma_{i,j} \frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial x_i} \frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial x_j} = \sum_{i=1}^d \sigma_{i,i} \left( \frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial x_i} \right)^2, \tag{13}
$$

since for $i \ne j$ , $\partial f/\partial x_i$ and $\partial f/\partial x_j$ live on disjoint sets. Now (13) is itself upper bounded by

$$
\max_{1\le i\le d} \sigma_{i,i} \sum_{i=1}^d \left( \frac{\partial f(x)}{\partial x_i} \right)^2 \le \max_{1\le i\le d} \sigma_{i,i} = \sigma^{*2}, \tag{14}
$$

since $\sum_{i=1}^d (\partial f/\partial x_i)^2 \le 1$ . There are a few ways to obtain this last fact: For example, if $e = (1,\dots,1) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ , and for $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ , $t \in \mathbb{R}$ , $\max(x + te) = (\max x) + t$ . Hence by the chain rule, $1 = d\max(x + te)/dt = \sum_{i=1}^d \partial \max x/\partial x_i$ .
